menu "KobitoKey Haptic"

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    bool "KobitoKey haptic motor support"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_KOBITOKEY_DRV2605L_ENABLED
    select I2C
    help
      The motor now lives behind a DRV2605L on i2c1, so this is only
      buildable where that node exists. Guarding it on the node keeps the
      shieldless builds -- settings_reset among them -- compiling.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_MS
    int "KobitoKey haptic cooldown in milliseconds"
    default 50
    range 0 2000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_DELAY_MS
    int "KobitoKey boot haptic delay in milliseconds"
    default 500
    range 0 10000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_GAP1_MS
    int "KobitoKey boot haptic first gap in milliseconds"
    default 600
    range 0 10000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_GAP2_MS
    int "KobitoKey boot haptic second gap in milliseconds"
    default 150
    range 0 10000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

# --- DRV2605L ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# The motor is now an LRA behind a DRV2605L rather than an ERM on a GPIO,
# so a buzz is "play effect N" instead of "hold the pin high for N ms".
# The chip ends the effect itself, which is why nothing here has to switch
# the motor back off.
#
# Effect numbers are the DRV2605 immersion library, with LIBRARY = 6 (LRA).
# A few useful ones: 1 strong click, 4 sharp click, 7 soft bump,
# 10 double click, 24 sharp tick 1, 47 buzz 1.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_EFFECT
    int "Effect played for pointer movement"
    default 24
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_EFFECT_LONG
    int "Effect played first at boot"
    default 47
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_EFFECT_SHORT
    int "Effect played for the two short boot buzzes"
    default 1
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_USB_EFFECT
    int "Effect played when USB is connected"
    default 1
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_RATED_VOLTAGE
    int "DRV2605L RATED_VOLTAGE register value"
    default 109
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Sets the steady-state drive level the closed loop aims for.

      The datasheet gives the RMS voltage produced by a given register
      value. An LRA is not driven for the whole period -- the driver stops
      to listen to the back-EMF -- so the RMS comes out below the drive
      amplitude by the square root of the fraction that is actually driven:

        Vrms = 20.71mV * RATED_VOLTAGE * sqrt(1 - 1.5ms * f_LRA)

      Going the other way, for the VLV041235L at 1.8 Vrms and 240 Hz:

        RATED_VOLTAGE = 1.8 / (0.02071 * 0.8) = 109

      This was 70 to begin with, from dividing where the equation
      multiplies. The motor ran at about two thirds of the voltage it is
      rated for, which is exactly what a weak buzz feels like.

      Raising it past 109 drives the motor harder than its rating.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_OD_CLAMP
    int "DRV2605L OD_CLAMP register value"
    default 175
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Ceiling on the drive voltage while overdriving.

      This is not the steady-state level -- the loop regulates that to
      RATED_VOLTAGE -- it is how hard the driver may hit the motor to get
      it moving and to brake it again. That overdrive is where the crispness
      of an LRA comes from, so clamping it close to the rated level takes
      away the very thing the DRV2605L is for.

      This is the knob for making everything stronger. A library click
      lasts tens of milliseconds and barely reaches steady state, so most
      of what the hand feels is the overdrive, not the rated level.

      Set by ratio rather than from the datasheet equation: the constant
      for the LRA form of that equation could not be read out of the
      document with confidence. 147 was a third above RATED_VOLTAGE; 175
      is roughly another fifth on top, reached by feel rather than by
      calculation, which is worth knowing before trusting the number.

      Raising it does not drive the motor past its rating in steady state
      -- the loop still regulates to RATED_VOLTAGE -- it only lets the
      driver hit harder on the way there and while braking.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BRAKE_FACTOR
    int "FB_BRAKE_FACTOR (0-7)"
    default 3
    range 0 7
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      0=1x 1=2x 2=3x 3=4x 4=6x 5=8x 6=16x 7=braking disabled.

      How hard the driver reverses to stop the mass. This is where the
      crispness of an LRA lives: the higher the factor, the shorter the
      ring-out.

      3 is 4x. 6 (16x) was tried and reverted -- not because it felt wrong
      but because it was changed at the same time as the loop gain and
      calibration started failing, so neither could be judged. Worth
      trying again on its own.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_LOOP_GAIN
    int "LOOP_GAIN (0-3)"
    default 1
    range 0 3
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      0=low 1=medium 2=high 3=very high. How aggressively the loop
      corrects towards the rated voltage.

      Medium, because it is what auto-calibration has actually succeeded
      under on this hardware. Low was tried, taken from a configuration
      that works for someone else with this motor, together with a 16x
      brake factor: calibration then failed on three boots out of three.
      That configuration assumes a 170 Hz actuator and this one does not
      behave like one, so its other numbers do not carry over either.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_DRIVE_TIME
    int "DRV2605L DRIVE_TIME field (CONTROL1 bits 4:0)"
    default 18
    range 0 31
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      How long the driver pushes before it stops to listen to the back-EMF.
      It has to line up with half the LRA's period, and the auto-calibration
      routine uses it as the starting point for finding resonance:

        drive time = 0.5ms + DRIVE_TIME * 0.1ms

      Measured rather than taken from the data sheet. The resonance
      register was read five times while the motor was being driven and
      returned period=46 every time, which is 4529us:

        DRIVE_TIME = (4529/2 - 500) / 100 = 18

      That is 220.79 Hz. The data sheet says 240 Hz and a configuration for
      this motor from elsewhere assumes 170 Hz; neither matches this build.
      Resonance moves with how the motor is mounted, so measuring beats
      copying.

      An earlier attempt read this register more than a second after
      playback had ended and got 236 Hz once and 423-508 Hz on other boots.
      The register is only meaningful while the loop is driving.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_AUTO_CAL_TIME
    int "DRV2605L AUTO_CAL_TIME (0-3)"
    default 3
    range 0 3
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      How long auto-calibration is allowed to drive the motor while it
      measures. 3 is the longest setting. Calibration happens once at boot,
      so there is little to gain by hurrying it and something to lose: a
      short window is one of the ways it fails to reach maximum back-EMF.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_AUTOCAL
    bool "Run DRV2605L auto-calibration at boot"
    default y
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Measures the motor and sets the compensation, back-EMF and gain
      registers. Takes well under a second and makes a short buzz of its
      own. The results are logged; once they are known and stable they can
      be written in directly and this turned off.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_COMP
    int "A_CAL_COMP written before any calibration"
    default 12
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_BEMF
    int "A_CAL_BEMF written before any calibration"
    default 109
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_BEMF_GAIN
    int "BEMF_GAIN (0-3) written before any calibration"
    default 2
    range 0 3
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      These three are the starting point every time the chip is brought
      up, and the only tuning the closed-lid USB buzz gets -- that path has
      no time for auto-calibration. Boot once with KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_AUTOCAL
      on, read the "Haptic calibrated" line out of the log, and copy the
      numbers here.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_PLAY_SETTLE_MS
    int "How long to wait for a boot-path buzz to finish"
    default 150
    range 10 1000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Only used on the closed-lid USB boot, where the half powers off as
      soon as it has read the lid. The GO bit is polled and this is just
      the ceiling, so a short effect returns long before it.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_SURVEY
    int "Repeat calibration this many times at boot and log each result"
    default 0
    range 0 20
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Diagnostic. 0 disables it.

      One passing calibration is not evidence that the calibration was
      right: this part has returned DIAG_RESULT=0 while reporting a
      back-EMF of 22 and a resonance of 508 Hz, which is roughly twice the
      real figure. Numbers taken from a single run are worth what that run
      happened to lock onto.

      Running it repeatedly and printing every result shows whether the
      measurement is repeatable at all. Tight cluster: trust it. Scatter:
      this actuator cannot be characterised this way and the drive should
      be fixed rather than measured.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_WORKQ_STACK_SIZE
    int "Stack size for the haptic thread"
    default 768
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Every register write happens on this thread, never on the input path
      and never on the system workqueue. A stuck bus then costs a buzz, not
      the pointer.

endmenu

